.TH GXWI-SYSTEM-SETTINGS 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-system-settings \- this machine's time, language, name and startup, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-system-settings
.SH DESCRIPTION
System Settings shows the machine's own settings in four sections down the
side. A switch or a choice from a list applies the moment it is made; one
that cannot be is put back, and the line along the bottom says why. What is
typed applies when its button is pressed: Apply, Save or Set Clock. What is
shown is read again every five seconds, so a change made elsewhere appears.
.PP
Time & Date shows the time here and how well it is kept, asked of timed. It
sets the time zone, whether the time is set automatically, the time servers
and how often they are asked, all in \%Machine\eSystem\eTime, which timed
watches. With automatic time off, Set Clock asks timed to set the clock,
which needs timed's control right.
.PP
Language & Keyboard sets the language and formats every session starts in,
in \%Machine\eSystem\eLocale, from the locales installed, and the console's
keyboard layout, in \%Machine\eSystem\eConsole. A new layout is loaded at once
by restarting the console-keymap service, where the person may restart it,
and otherwise at the next boot.
.PP
Startup & Shutdown says how this boot went, asked of peinit. It sets how
long peinit waits at each stage, in \%Machine\eSystem\eBoot; which local
account, if any, the console signs in without a password; and, on a machine
with the dynamic-boot feature, three flags on the kernel command line the
next boot image is made from: when to start in recovery, what peinit writes
to the console, and whether every boot starts in safe mode.
.PP
About shows the release, the kernel, the hardware and the disks, and sets
the machine's name, in \%Machine\eSystem\eNetwork, which netd applies.
.PP
Whether the person may change each is asked of the system, by opening the
key or file for writing, never worked out from who they are. As shipped,
only Administrators may change any of it. Anyone else sees what they may,
and the window says once why they cannot change it. A person's own settings are
My Settings', and the desktop's are Desktop Settings'.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-system-settings.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as System Settings.
.TP
.I /lcl/etc/boot/cmdline
The kernel command line the next boot image is made from.
.TP
.I /usr/share/zoneinfo/zone1970.tab
The time zones offered.
.TP
.I /usr/share/kbd/keymaps/i386
The console keyboard layouts offered.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-peios/locale
The languages and formats offered.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/os-release
The release shown under About.
.SH SEE ALSO
The time zone, and setting the clock by hand; Configuring time sources;
Session locales; Console keyboards and fonts; Boot and boot modes; and The
login command, at learn.peios.org.
